%erg v1
Title: Rename Tags: to Tag: if only one value is meaningful per ticket
Created: 2026-05-07
Author: claude-sonnet-4-6

--- log ---
2026-05-07T00:00Z claude-sonnet-4-6 created

--- body ---
## Context
The spec marks `Tags` as repeatable (`yes`), but all four allowed values
(`needs-human`, `deferred`, `post-talk`, `post-conference`) are independent
flags — there is no natural reason to apply more than one to a ticket at the
same time.  A plural field name with a singular-value constraint is confusing.
If the intent is "at most one tag", the field should be `Tag:` (singular,
non-repeatable).

## Actions
1. Audit all existing `.erg` files: count how many have more than one `Tags:`
   line.
2. Decide: if no ticket has multiple tags, change the field to singular `Tag:`
   (non-repeatable) in both spec and code.  If multiple tags are genuinely
   used, keep `Tags:` but document the multi-value intent explicitly.
3. If renaming: update `tickets/spec-erg-v1.md`, the validator
   (`validate.go`), the parser (`ticket.go`), the `Erg.Tags()` method (→
   `Tag()`), all tests, and any shell scripts that grep for `Tags:`.
4. Bump the format version or add a migration note if the rename is
   backward-incompatible.

## Test
```bash
grep -r '^Tags:' tickets/ | wc -l   # should be 0 after rename
grep -r '^Tag:' tickets/ | wc -l    # new field
```
Write a validator test that rejects a file with two `Tag:` lines if the field
is declared non-repeatable.

## Exit criteria
- Field name and repeatability in spec, code, and tests are consistent.
- `make test` passes.
- Existing tickets are migrated (or a migration script is provided).
- Decision (rename vs. keep) is documented in the PR body with the audit
  count.
